{"type":"message","id":"eb988cc5","parentId":"280fdb35","timestamp":"2026-02-01T04:04:13.658Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01W1pBNPLCgnfKWrjByvx9VD","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cat /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md 2>/dev/null; cat /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/STATE.md 2>/dev/null; ls /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/*.md 2>/dev/null"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01XVBEbRYchvywZokYBoV3jd","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"ls /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/polymarket-copytrade/data/ 2>/dev/null; cat /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/polymarket-copytrade/data/daily-report-latest.txt 2>/dev/null | head -40"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"anthropic","model":"claude-opus-4-5","usage":{"input":1,"output":257,"cacheRead":11228,"cacheWrite":2337,"totalTokens":13823,"cost":{"input":0.000005,"output":0.006425,"cacheRead":0.005614,"cacheWrite":0.014606250000000001,"total":0.02665025}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1769918649775}}
{"type":"message","id":"ef780516","parentId":"eb988cc5","timestamp":"2026-02-01T04:04:13.691Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_01W1pBNPLCgnfKWrjByvx9VD","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Team of Rivals — Multi-Agent Bid Takeoff Architecture\n## HHC South Brooklyn Health, Dental Suite 5th Floor | Project #26202103\n\n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Date:** 2025-07-11  \n**Reference:** arxiv.org/abs/2601.14351 (specialized agent teams with opposing incentives)\n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nWe have 298 bid line items, 86 drawing sheets, a 1,058-page spec, and most quantities still unmeasured. The single-agent approach can't hold enough context, can't self-check, and produces errors like the GWB layer discrepancy (Item 51) and the missing fire-rated door. This architecture splits the work across four specialized agents with adversarial incentives—each agent's success metric conflicts with at least one other agent's, ensuring errors get caught.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Agent Roles\n\n### 1A. PLANNER (Orchestrator) — `model: kimi`\n\n**Role:** Decomposes bid line items into discrete tasks, sequences work, manages dependencies, tracks progress.\n\n**Model:** moonshot/kimi-k2.5 — long context, structured output, cost-efficient.\n\n**Incentive:** Throughput — measured by items completed per cycle.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Read `bid_breakdown.json` and `takeoff_checklist.md` to identify next batch of unmeasured items\n- Group items by drawing sheet (e.g., all items needing A-100.00 go together)\n- Create task packets for Executor with minimal, relevant context\n- Track completion status in `takeoff_progress.json`\n- Decide when to escalate to Doug vs. auto-resolve\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are the Planner for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff (Project #26202103).\n\nCONTEXT FILES (read these):\n- bid_breakdown.json (298 line items, current quantities)\n- takeoff_progress.json (completion status)\n- takeoff_checklist.md (known gaps and flags)\n\nYOUR JOB: Select the next batch of 5-10 bid line items to quantify. For each item, produce a TASK PACKET:\n\n{\n  \"item_number\": <int>,\n  \"description\": \"<from bid breakdown>\",\n  \"unit\": \"<SF/LF/EA/LS>\",\n  \"sheets_needed\": [\"<sheet IDs from drawing_index.json>\"],\n  \"spec_sections\": [\"<section numbers from specs_summary.json>\"],\n  \"context_summary\": \"<1-2 sentences of relevant info from scope_analysis.md>\",\n  \"known_flags\": [\"<any flags from takeoff_checklist.md>\"],\n  \"depends_on\": [<item numbers that must be done first, if any>]\n}\n\nRULES:\n- Group items that share the same drawing sheet\n- Prioritize Doug's direct scope (Demo, Finishes, Specialties, Openings, Carpentry)\n- Do NOT include raw spec text — summaries only\n- Flag items with known discrepancies for Critic review\n\nOutput: JSON array of task packets + updated priority queue.\n```\n\n### 1B. EXECUTOR (Quantity Takeoff Agent) — `model: kimi`\n\n**Role:** Reads drawing sheets, measures quantities, maps to bid line items.\n\n**Model:** moonshot/kimi-k2.5 — bulk data extraction at low cost. Falls back to gpt4o for image-heavy sheets.\n\n**Incentive:** Accuracy of measurements — penalized for quantities that Critic rejects.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Receive task packet from Planner\n- Read specified drawing sheet images (PNG from `/sheets/`)\n- Extract quantities: count items (EA), measure linear footage (LF), calculate areas (SF)\n- Cross-reference room_schedule.json and finish_schedule.json for area validation\n- Produce structured quantity output with measurement methodology notes\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are the Executor for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff.\n\nTASK PACKET:\n{task_packet_json}\n\nREFERENCE DATA (loaded for you):\n- Drawing sheet image(s): {sheet_paths}\n- room_schedule.json (room names, areas, finishes)\n- finish_schedule.json (finish types by room)\n- partition_schedule.json (partition type assemblies)\n- door_schedule.json (door types, sizes, ratings)\n\nYOUR JOB: Measure the quantity for each bid item in the task packet.\n\nFor EACH item, output:\n{\n  \"item_number\": <int>,\n  \"quantity\": <number>,\n  \"unit\": \"<SF/LF/EA>\",\n  \"measurement_method\": \"<how you derived this — which rooms, what dimensions, what formula>\",\n  \"sheets_used\": [\"<which sheets you actually read>\"],\n  \"confidence\": \"high|medium|low\",\n  \"assumptions\": [\"<any assumptions made>\"],\n  \"cross_check\": \"<sanity check — e.g., 'total partition SF consistent with ~3,500 SF suite area'>\",\n  \"flags\": [\"<anything unexpected>\"]\n}\n\nRULES:\n- Show your math. Every quantity must have a derivation.\n- If a drawing is illegible or ambiguous, say so — don't guess.\n- Use room_schedule areas as sanity checks, NOT as primary measurements.\n- Flag any discrepancy between what you see on drawings and what the bid breakdown describes.\n- For partitions: measure EACH FACE separately (two finishes = two measurements).\n- For doors: note size, rating, and hardware set from schedule.\n```\n\n### 1C. CRITIC (Adversarial QA Agent) — `model: o3mini`\n\n**Role:** Reviews Executor output for errors, omissions, inconsistencies. Actively tries to find problems.\n\n**Model:** openai/o3-mini — strong math/logic reasoning, cost-efficient for verification tasks.\n\n**Incentive:** Error detection rate — rewarded for finding real errors, penalized for false positives.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Cross-reference quantities against specs, drawings, and bid breakdown\n- Check unit consistency (SF vs LF vs EA)\n- Verify code compliance (fire ratings, ADA, ICRA)\n- Look for missing items (things on drawings not in bid)\n- Challenge Executor's assumptions and math\n- Produce accept/reject/flag decisions\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are the Critic for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff. Your job is to FIND ERRORS.\n\nEXECUTOR OUTPUT:\n{executor_output_json}\n\nORIGINAL TASK PACKET:\n{task_packet_json}\n\nREFERENCE DATA:\n- specs_summary.json (spec requirements by section)\n- partition_schedule.json (assembly details — layers, ratings)\n- door_schedule.json (sizes, ratings, hardware sets)\n- scope_analysis.md § \"CRITICAL FLAGS SUMMARY\"\n- Known discrepancies from takeoff_checklist.md\n\nRUN THESE CHECKS (all of them, every time):\n\n1. UNIT CONSISTENCY\n   - Does the measured unit match the bid breakdown unit?\n   - Is SF used where LF should be (or vice versa)?\n   - Flag: Item 58 (epoxy floor) is LF — verify this is correct\n\n2. SPEC vs DRAWING CROSS-REFERENCE\n   - Does the spec require something not reflected in the quantity?\n   - Example: Spec 092900 requires Type X GWB for fire-rated assemblies — did Executor account for 2 layers?\n   - Example: Spec 093013 says \"no substitutions\" on tile — is the specified product noted?\n\n3. MISSING ITEMS\n   - Is there anything visible on the drawing that has NO corresponding bid line item?\n   - Example: Fire-rated door at Mechanical Room — is it in the bid?\n   - Example: Lead-lined GWB at Panorex room — is it in the bid?\n\n4. CODE COMPLIANCE\n   - Fire-rated partitions: correct UL assembly? Correct number of GWB layers?\n   - ADA: grab bars at all required locations? Clearances?\n   - ICRA: dust control and infection barriers accounted for?\n\n5. MATH VERIFICATION\n   - Recalculate at least 2 quantities independently\n   - Check that room areas sum to approximately suite total (~3,500 SF)\n   - Check that partition SF is reasonable for the suite size\n\n6. KNOWN ISSUES CHECK\n   - Item 51 GWB layer discrepancy — is this addressed?\n   - Fire-rated door missing from bid — is this flagged?\n   - Altro Whitrock vs ceramic tile overlap — is this clarified?\n\nOUTPUT for each item:\n{\n  \"item_number\": <int>,\n  \"verdict\": \"ACCEPT|REJECT|FLAG\",\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"CRITICAL|WARNING|INFO\",\n      \"check\": \"<which check caught this>\",\n      \"description\": \"<what's wrong>\",\n      \"evidence\": \"<spec section, drawing reference, or math>\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"<fix or escalate>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"revised_quantity\": <number or null if ACCEPT>,\n  \"escalate_to_doug\": <boolean>\n}\n```\n\n### 1D. DOMAIN EXPERT (Spec & Code Specialist)\n\n**Role:** Deep-dives into specific technical questions that Planner/Executor/Critic can't resolve. Called on-demand, not every cycle.\n\n**Incentive:** Resolution quality — measured by whether escalated issues get resolved without re-escalation.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Answer specific technical questions about specs, codes, UL assemblies\n- Resolve ambiguities between drawings and specifications\n- Provide manufacturer/product-specific guidance\n- Draft RFI language when clarification needed from architect\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are a Domain Expert for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff. You specialize in {domain}.\n\nQUESTION:\n{specific_question}\n\nRELEVANT SPEC TEXT:\n{spec_excerpt — max 2000 chars}\n\nRELEVANT DRAWING DATA:\n{drawing_notes_or_schedule_excerpt}\n\nPROJECT CONTEXT:\n- Healthcare dental clinic renovation, 5th floor, occupied hospital\n- NYC building code applies\n- Architect: Mancini Duffy\n- Single prime contract\n\nProvide:\n1. ANSWER: Clear, definitive answer with code/spec citations\n2. CONFIDENCE: How certain are you? (high/medium/low)\n3. IF LOW CONFIDENCE: Draft RFI question for the architect\n4. PRICING IMPACT: Does this change the bid quantity or cost? How?\n```\n\n**Domain specializations (spawn the right one):**\n- `fire-protection`: UL assemblies, NFPA 80, fire ratings, firestopping\n- `finishes`: GWB types, tile, flooring, paint systems, healthcare finishes\n- `doors-hardware`: hollow metal, hardware sets, keying, access control\n- `healthcare-code`: ICRA, infection control, ADA, NYC DOB requirements\n- `dental-equipment`: Henry Schein coordination, dental air/vacuum, X-ray shielding\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workflow Pipeline\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    PLANNER                                │\n│  Reads: bid_breakdown.json, takeoff_progress.json        │\n│  Outputs: Task Packets (batches of 5-10 items)           │\n└────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘\n                     │ Task Packet\n                     ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    EXECUTOR                               │\n│  Reads: Drawing PNGs, room/finish/partition/door JSONs   │\n│  Outputs: Quantities + measurement methodology           │\n└────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘\n                     │ Executor Output\n                     ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    CRITIC                                 │\n│  Reads: Executor output + specs + known flags            │\n│  Outputs: ACCEPT / REJECT / FLAG per item                │\n└──────┬─────────────┬──────────────────┬─────────────────┘\n       │             │                  │\n  ACCEPT         REJECT              FLAG\n       │             │                  │\n       ▼             ▼                  ▼\n  ┌─────────┐  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐\n  │ Commit   │  │ Back to  │    │ DOMAIN       │\n  │ to JSON  │  │ Executor │    │ EXPERT       │\n  │          │  │ (retry)  │    │ (on-demand)  │\n  └─────────┘  └──────────┘    └──────┬───────┘\n                                      │\n                               ┌──────┴───────┐\n                               │  Resolved?   │\n                               ├──Yes──┐      │\n                               │       ▼      │No\n                               │   Commit     │\n                               │              ▼\n                               │     ESCALATE TO DOUG\n                               └──────────────┘\n```\n\n### Step-by-Step for a Single Bid Line Item\n\n**Example: Item 50 — W32 partition, 3-5/8\" studs, 1×5/8\" GWB ea side, non-rated**\n\n1. **Planner** creates task packet:\n   - Sheets needed: A-100.00 (construction plan, part3/p12)\n   - Spec sections: 092216 (metal framing), 092900 (GWB)\n   - Context: \"Non-rated partition type W3. Measure total LF from construction plan, multiply by wall height for SF. Note: W2 is the 1-HR rated variant — don't confuse them.\"\n   - Known flags: \"Item 51 has GWB layer discrepancy for W2 type\"\n\n2. **Executor** reads sheet part3/page-12 (A-100.00):\n   - Identifies all W3-type partition segments on plan\n   - Measures linear footage of each segment\n   - Multiplies by ceiling height (likely 9'-0\" or per partition schedule)\n   - Calculates SF for both faces\n   - Output: `{ quantity: 1240, unit: \"SF\", method: \"7 segments totaling 138 LF × 9' height = 1,242 SF, rounded to 1,240\", confidence: \"medium\" }`\n\n3. **Critic** reviews:\n   - ✅ Unit matches bid (SF)\n   - ✅ Math checks out (138 × 9 ≈ 1,242)\n   - ⚠️ WARNING: \"Executor measured total SF but bid may want per-face SF. Clarify: is 1,240 SF one face or both?\"\n   - ✅ No spec conflicts for non-rated assembly\n   - Verdict: FLAG — needs clarification on face count\n\n4. **Domain Expert** (finishes) resolves:\n   - \"Industry standard for partition SF in bid breakdowns: measure one face. The bid line item covers the assembly, not individual faces. 1,240 SF = one face is correct if that's the total surface area of one side.\"\n   - Resolved → Commit\n\n5. **Planner** updates `takeoff_progress.json`:\n   - Item 50: `{ status: \"complete\", quantity: 1240, unit: \"SF\", reviewed: true }`\n\n---\n\n## 3. Adversarial Checks (Critic Agent)\n\n### Check Matrix\n\n| # | Check Name | What It Catches | Example from This Project |\n|---|-----------|-----------------|--------------------------|\n| 1 | **Unit Mismatch** | Wrong measurement unit | Item 58: epoxy floor in LF (should be SF?) |\n| 2 | **Layer Count** | Missing GWB layers in rated assemblies | Item 51: bid says 1 layer, UL requires 2 for 1-HR |\n| 3 | **Missing Scope** | Items on drawings not in bid | Fire-rated door at Mech Room (Door 1 = ¾-HR) |\n| 4 | **Spec Conflict** | Drawing ≠ spec requirement | No-sub clause on tile (093013) — must price exact product |\n| 5 | **Double Count** | Same work counted in two items | Rough carpentry blocking vs. wall protection backing |\n| 6 | **Area Sanity** | Total areas exceed suite footprint | Sum of all floor finishes > ~3,500 SF = problem |\n| 7 | **Code Compliance** | Missing code-required items | Lead-lined GWB at Panorex X-ray room |\n| 8 | **Orphan Items** | Bid items with no drawing reference | Items that can't be traced to any sheet |\n| 9 | **Price Reasonableness** | Quantities that imply absurd costs | 10,000 SF of partition in a 3,500 SF suite |\n| 10 | **Coordination Gap** | MEP/arch conflicts | Dental chair rough-in vs. Henry Schein coordination |\n\n### Cross-Reference Matrix (Critic must verify these intersections)\n\n```\n                    Drawings  Specs   Bid Breakdown  Schedules\nDrawings              —        ✓          ✓             ✓\nSpecs                 ✓        —          ✓             ✓\nBid Breakdown         ✓        ✓          —             ✓\nSchedules             ✓        ✓          ✓             —\n```\n\nFor every item, Critic checks at minimum 2 of 4 sources agree on: quantity unit, scope inclusion, and material specification.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Error Interception Rules\n\n### Decision Tree\n\n```\nERROR DETECTED\n    │\n    ├── Is it a UNIT MISMATCH (SF/LF/EA)?\n    │     └── AUTO-RESOLVE if obvious (e.g., \"LF\" for base = correct)\n    │         FLAG if ambiguous (e.g., Item 58 epoxy \"LF\" — could be either)\n    │\n    ├── Is it a MATH ERROR (wrong multiplication/addition)?\n    │     └── AUTO-RESOLVE: Critic recalculates, Executor retries\n    │\n    ├── Is it a MISSING ITEM (on drawing, not in bid)?\n    │     └── ESCALATE TO DOUG — he decides if it's an RFI or an add\n    │\n    ├── Is it a SPEC vs DRAWING CONFLICT?\n    │     ├── Minor (paint sheen, sealant type): FLAG, note in output\n    │     └── Major (fire rating, structural): ESCALATE TO DOUG\n    │\n    ├── Is it a CODE COMPLIANCE issue?\n    │     └── ALWAYS ESCALATE TO DOUG\n    │\n    └── Is the Executor CONFIDENCE \"low\"?\n          └── DOMAIN EXPERT review before committing\n```\n\n### Severity Definitions\n\n| Level | Definition | Action | Example |\n|-------|-----------|--------|---------|\n| **CRITICAL** | Wrong quantity could lose the bid or create liability | STOP. Escalate to Doug immediately. | Fire-rated door missing, GWB layer discrepancy |\n| **WARNING** | Likely error but bounded financial impact | Domain Expert review, then commit or escalate | Unit ambiguity on rubber base (EA vs LF) |\n| **INFO** | Notable but not blocking | Log it, continue | Paint color code confirmation |\n\n### Auto-Resolve Conditions (no human needed)\n\n1. Math error where Critic's recalculation matches a known reference (room_schedule area)\n2. Obvious unit mapping (e.g., doors = EA, paint = SF, base = LF)\n3. Duplicate detection where one item clearly supersedes another\n4. Rounding differences < 5%\n\n### Escalate-to-Doug Triggers\n\n1. Any CRITICAL severity finding\n2. Spec vs drawing conflict on fire-rated assemblies\n3. Missing bid line item for scope visible on drawings\n4. Healthcare code compliance question (ICRA, X-ray shielding, ADA)\n5. Quantity confidence \"low\" after Domain Expert review\n6. Any item where financial impact > $5,000 estimated\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n\n### 5A. File Structure\n\n```\nhhc-brooklyn-dental/\n├── TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md          ← this file\n├── takeoff_progress.json            ← master tracking (Planner updates)\n├── bid_breakdown.json               ← 298 items (source of truth)\n├── scope_analysis.md                ← project context summary\n├── takeoff_checklist.md             ← gap analysis\n├── agents/\n│   ├── planner_output/              ← task packets per batch\n│   │   ├── batch-001.json\n│   │   └── batch-002.json\n│   ├── executor_output/             ← quantity results per batch\n│   │   ├── batch-001-quantities.json\n│   │   └── batch-002-quantities.json\n│   ├── critic_output/               ← QA verdicts per batch\n│   │   ├── batch-001-review.json\n│   │   └── batch-002-review.json\n│   └── expert_output/               ← domain expert consultations\n│       ├── fire-rating-item51.json\n│       └── missing-door-item44.json\n├── escalations/                     ← items needing Doug's input\n│   ├── gwb-layer-discrepancy.md\n│   └── fire-rated-door-missing.md\n└── final/\n    └── completed_takeoff.json       ← final quantities, QA-passed\n```\n\n### 5B. Session Spawn Commands\n\nEach agent runs as a spawned subagent session. The main session (or a cron trigger) acts as the Planner, spawning Executor and Critic in sequence.\n\n#### Spawn Planner (or run in main session)\n```\nTask: Run the Planner role for HHC Brooklyn Dental takeoff.\n\nRead these files:\n- /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/bid_breakdown.json\n- /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/takeoff_progress.json\n- /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/takeoff_checklist.md\n\nSelect the next batch of 5-10 unmeasured bid items from Doug's direct scope.\nGroup by drawing sheet. Output task packets to agents/planner_output/batch-{NNN}.json.\nUpdate takeoff_progress.json with status \"in-progress\" for selected items.\n```\n\n#### Spawn Executor\n```\nTask: Execute quantity takeoff for HHC Brooklyn Dental.\n\nRead your task packet: agents/planner_output/batch-{NNN}.json\n\nFor each item in the packet:\n1. Read the specified drawing sheet image(s) from /sheets/\n2. Read reference data: room_schedule.json, finish_schedule.json, partition_schedule.json, door_schedule.json\n3. Measure quantities per the Executor prompt template in TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md § 1B\n4. Write results to agents/executor_output/batch-{NNN}-quantities.json\n\nShow your work. Every number needs a derivation.\n```\n\n#### Spawn Critic\n```\nTask: QA review of quantity takeoff for HHC Brooklyn Dental.\n\nRead:\n- Executor output: agents/executor_output/batch-{NNN}-quantities.json\n- Original task packet: agents/planner_output/batch-{NNN}.json\n- specs_summary.json, partition_schedule.json, door_schedule.json\n- scope_analysis.md (Critical Flags section)\n- takeoff_checklist.md (Known Discrepancies section)\n\nRun ALL checks from TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md § 1C and § 3.\nOutput verdicts to agents/critic_output/batch-{NNN}-review.json.\n\nYour job is to find errors. Be adversarial. Challenge every assumption.\nIf something seems too convenient or too round, dig deeper.\n```\n\n#### Spawn Domain Expert (on-demand)\n```\nTask: Resolve technical question for HHC Brooklyn Dental takeoff.\n\nDomain: {fire-protection|finishes|doors-hardware|healthcare-code|dental-equipment}\n\nQuestion: {specific_question}\n\nRead relevant spec section from specs_summary.json (section {NNN}).\nRead relevant schedule from {schedule_file}.json.\n\nFollow the Domain Expert prompt template in TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md § 1D.\nWrite resolution to agents/expert_output/{topic-slug}.json.\n```\n\n### 5C. Orchestration Flow (Main Session Script)\n\n```\nBATCH_CYCLE:\n1. Run Planner → produces batch-{N}.json\n2. Spawn Executor subagent with batch-{N}\n3. Wait for Executor completion\n4. Spawn Critic subagent with batch-{N} results\n5. Wait for Critic completion\n6. Parse Critic output:\n   - ACCEPT items → write to final/completed_takeoff.json\n   - REJECT items → re-queue for Executor (max 2 retries)\n   - FLAG items → spawn Domain Expert OR write to escalations/\n7. Update takeoff_progress.json\n8. If items remain → goto BATCH_CYCLE\n9. When all items complete → generate final summary for Doug\n```\n\n### 5D. Progress Tracking Schema\n\n`takeoff_progress.json`:\n```json\n{\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"total_items\": 298,\n    \"dougs_scope_items\": 82,\n    \"last_updated\": \"2025-07-11T00:00:00Z\"\n  },\n  \"items\": {\n    \"50\": {\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"quantity\": 1240,\n      \"unit\": \"SF\",\n      \"batch\": \"batch-003\",\n      \"executor_confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"critic_verdict\": \"ACCEPT\",\n      \"retries\": 0,\n      \"completed_at\": \"2025-07-11T12:00:00Z\"\n    },\n    \"51\": {\n      \"status\": \"escalated\",\n      \"batch\": \"batch-003\",\n      \"escalation_reason\": \"GWB layer count discrepancy — bid says 1 layer, UL U419 requires 2\",\n      \"escalation_file\": \"escalations/gwb-layer-discrepancy.md\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"complete\": 0,\n    \"in_progress\": 0,\n    \"escalated\": 2,\n    \"not_started\": 296\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Data Hygiene\n\n### The Problem\n\nThe spec PDF is 1,058 pages. The drawings are 86 sheets. Dumping raw content into agent context will:\n- Blow token limits\n- Dilute signal with noise\n- Cause hallucinations from irrelevant text\n\n### Rules\n\n| Rule | Implementation |\n|------|---------------|\n| **No raw PDF text in agent context** | Agents read `specs_summary.json` (pre-extracted, 21 sections with metadata). Full text only via Domain Expert on specific sections. |\n| **No full drawing sets** | Executor gets only the 1-3 sheet PNGs specified in the task packet. Never all 86. |\n| **Summaries over source** | Planner provides 1-2 sentence context summaries per item, not full spec paragraphs. |\n| **Structured data only** | All inter-agent communication via JSON files in `agents/` directory. No prose dumps. |\n| **Context ceiling: 8K tokens per agent** | Task packets, reference data, and instructions must fit in ~8K tokens. If they don't, split the batch. |\n| **Immutable source files** | `bid_breakdown.json`, `specs_summary.json`, schedules — agents READ these, never WRITE. Only `takeoff_progress.json` and `agents/` output files are writable. |\n\n### Pre-Extracted Reference Data (already exists)\n\n| File | Contents | Size | Agent Access |\n|------|----------|------|-------------|\n| `bid_breakdown.json` | 298 line items (no quantities yet) | Small | Planner |\n| `specs_summary.json` | 21 spec sections with titles, page ranges, manufacturers | Medium | Critic, Expert |\n| `room_schedule.json` | 30 rooms with areas and finish codes | Small | Executor |\n| `finish_schedule.json` | Finish types mapped to rooms | Small | Executor |\n| `partition_schedule.json` | Partition assembly details (W1-W4, F1, C00) | Small | Executor, Critic |\n| `door_schedule.json` | 25 doors with sizes, ratings, hardware | Small | Executor, Critic |\n| `drawing_index.json` | Sheet catalog with page references | Small | Planner |\n| `sheet_index.json` | 80 sheets with text previews | Medium | Planner |\n| `scope_analysis.md` | Full project analysis with flags | Large | Planner (full), others (flags section only) |\n\n### What Needs Creating\n\n| File | Purpose | Created By |\n|------|---------|-----------|\n| `takeoff_progress.json` | Master status tracker | Planner (initialize from bid_breakdown) |\n| `agents/` directory tree | Inter-agent communication | First Planner run |\n| `escalations/` directory | Doug review queue | Critic/Expert when escalating |\n| `final/completed_takeoff.json` | Accepted quantities | Orchestrator after Critic ACCEPT |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Known Issues to Pre-Seed\n\nThese issues from `takeoff_checklist.md` should be immediately routed to the appropriate agent on first run:\n\n| Issue | Route To | Priority |\n|-------|----------|----------|\n| Item 51 — GWB 1-HR partition layer count | Domain Expert (fire-protection) → Escalate to Doug | 🔴 CRITICAL |\n| Fire-rated door missing from bid (Door 1 = ¾-HR) | Critic → Escalate to Doug | 🔴 CRITICAL |\n| Item 58 — Epoxy floor unit is LF not SF | Critic → Domain Expert (finishes) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Item 61 — Rubber base unit is EA | Critic → Domain Expert (finishes) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Lead-lined GWB at Panorex room | Domain Expert (healthcare-code) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Altro Whitrock vs ceramic tile scope overlap | Domain Expert (finishes) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Henry Schein dental equipment coordination | Domain Expert (dental-equipment) → Escalate to Doug | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Hazmat/ACM abatement scope unknown (Item 7) | Escalate to Doug directly | 🔴 CRITICAL |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target | Measurement |\n|--------|--------|-------------|\n| Items completed per batch cycle | 5-10 | `takeoff_progress.json` |\n| Critic rejection rate | < 30% (improving over time) | Reject count / total reviewed |\n| False positive rate (Critic flags that aren't real) | < 15% | Doug overrides / total flags |\n| Time to complete Doug's scope (82 items) | 4-6 batch cycles | Progress tracking |\n| Escalations to Doug | < 10 total | Escalation directory count |\n| Zero missed scope items | 0 items on drawings not in bid (after full run) | Final audit |\n\n---\n\n## Appendix: Adversarial Incentive Structure\n\nPer arxiv.org/abs/2601.14351, opposing incentives prevent groupthink:\n\n| Agent | Wants | Opposes |\n|-------|-------|---------|\n| **Planner** | High throughput (more items done) | Critic (slows pipeline with rejections) |\n| **Executor** | High acceptance rate | Critic (rejects sloppy work) |\n| **Critic** | High error detection | Executor (wants clean pass), Planner (wants speed) |\n| **Domain Expert** | Definitive resolution | Critic (may re-flag), Planner (slows queue) |\n\nNo single agent can declare \"done\" — the Critic must approve, and the Critic is incentivized to find problems. The Planner is incentivized to move fast, creating natural tension that surfaces issues early.\n\n---\n\n*This architecture replaces the single-agent takeoff approach. First batch cycle should target Items 8-23 (Demolition) as these are prerequisite to all other quantities.*\n/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/DEMO-TAKEOFF-WORKSHEET.md\n/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md\n/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/scope_analysis.md\n/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/takeoff_checklist.md"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":33,"aggregated":"# Team of Rivals — Multi-Agent Bid Takeoff Architecture\n## HHC South Brooklyn Health, Dental Suite 5th Floor | Project #26202103\n\n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Date:** 2025-07-11  \n**Reference:** arxiv.org/abs/2601.14351 (specialized agent teams with opposing incentives)\n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nWe have 298 bid line items, 86 drawing sheets, a 1,058-page spec, and most quantities still unmeasured. The single-agent approach can't hold enough context, can't self-check, and produces errors like the GWB layer discrepancy (Item 51) and the missing fire-rated door. This architecture splits the work across four specialized agents with adversarial incentives—each agent's success metric conflicts with at least one other agent's, ensuring errors get caught.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Agent Roles\n\n### 1A. PLANNER (Orchestrator) — `model: kimi`\n\n**Role:** Decomposes bid line items into discrete tasks, sequences work, manages dependencies, tracks progress.\n\n**Model:** moonshot/kimi-k2.5 — long context, structured output, cost-efficient.\n\n**Incentive:** Throughput — measured by items completed per cycle.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Read `bid_breakdown.json` and `takeoff_checklist.md` to identify next batch of unmeasured items\n- Group items by drawing sheet (e.g., all items needing A-100.00 go together)\n- Create task packets for Executor with minimal, relevant context\n- Track completion status in `takeoff_progress.json`\n- Decide when to escalate to Doug vs. auto-resolve\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are the Planner for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff (Project #26202103).\n\nCONTEXT FILES (read these):\n- bid_breakdown.json (298 line items, current quantities)\n- takeoff_progress.json (completion status)\n- takeoff_checklist.md (known gaps and flags)\n\nYOUR JOB: Select the next batch of 5-10 bid line items to quantify. For each item, produce a TASK PACKET:\n\n{\n  \"item_number\": <int>,\n  \"description\": \"<from bid breakdown>\",\n  \"unit\": \"<SF/LF/EA/LS>\",\n  \"sheets_needed\": [\"<sheet IDs from drawing_index.json>\"],\n  \"spec_sections\": [\"<section numbers from specs_summary.json>\"],\n  \"context_summary\": \"<1-2 sentences of relevant info from scope_analysis.md>\",\n  \"known_flags\": [\"<any flags from takeoff_checklist.md>\"],\n  \"depends_on\": [<item numbers that must be done first, if any>]\n}\n\nRULES:\n- Group items that share the same drawing sheet\n- Prioritize Doug's direct scope (Demo, Finishes, Specialties, Openings, Carpentry)\n- Do NOT include raw spec text — summaries only\n- Flag items with known discrepancies for Critic review\n\nOutput: JSON array of task packets + updated priority queue.\n```\n\n### 1B. EXECUTOR (Quantity Takeoff Agent) — `model: kimi`\n\n**Role:** Reads drawing sheets, measures quantities, maps to bid line items.\n\n**Model:** moonshot/kimi-k2.5 — bulk data extraction at low cost. Falls back to gpt4o for image-heavy sheets.\n\n**Incentive:** Accuracy of measurements — penalized for quantities that Critic rejects.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Receive task packet from Planner\n- Read specified drawing sheet images (PNG from `/sheets/`)\n- Extract quantities: count items (EA), measure linear footage (LF), calculate areas (SF)\n- Cross-reference room_schedule.json and finish_schedule.json for area validation\n- Produce structured quantity output with measurement methodology notes\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are the Executor for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff.\n\nTASK PACKET:\n{task_packet_json}\n\nREFERENCE DATA (loaded for you):\n- Drawing sheet image(s): {sheet_paths}\n- room_schedule.json (room names, areas, finishes)\n- finish_schedule.json (finish types by room)\n- partition_schedule.json (partition type assemblies)\n- door_schedule.json (door types, sizes, ratings)\n\nYOUR JOB: Measure the quantity for each bid item in the task packet.\n\nFor EACH item, output:\n{\n  \"item_number\": <int>,\n  \"quantity\": <number>,\n  \"unit\": \"<SF/LF/EA>\",\n  \"measurement_method\": \"<how you derived this — which rooms, what dimensions, what formula>\",\n  \"sheets_used\": [\"<which sheets you actually read>\"],\n  \"confidence\": \"high|medium|low\",\n  \"assumptions\": [\"<any assumptions made>\"],\n  \"cross_check\": \"<sanity check — e.g., 'total partition SF consistent with ~3,500 SF suite area'>\",\n  \"flags\": [\"<anything unexpected>\"]\n}\n\nRULES:\n- Show your math. Every quantity must have a derivation.\n- If a drawing is illegible or ambiguous, say so — don't guess.\n- Use room_schedule areas as sanity checks, NOT as primary measurements.\n- Flag any discrepancy between what you see on drawings and what the bid breakdown describes.\n- For partitions: measure EACH FACE separately (two finishes = two measurements).\n- For doors: note size, rating, and hardware set from schedule.\n```\n\n### 1C. CRITIC (Adversarial QA Agent) — `model: o3mini`\n\n**Role:** Reviews Executor output for errors, omissions, inconsistencies. Actively tries to find problems.\n\n**Model:** openai/o3-mini — strong math/logic reasoning, cost-efficient for verification tasks.\n\n**Incentive:** Error detection rate — rewarded for finding real errors, penalized for false positives.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Cross-reference quantities against specs, drawings, and bid breakdown\n- Check unit consistency (SF vs LF vs EA)\n- Verify code compliance (fire ratings, ADA, ICRA)\n- Look for missing items (things on drawings not in bid)\n- Challenge Executor's assumptions and math\n- Produce accept/reject/flag decisions\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are the Critic for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff. Your job is to FIND ERRORS.\n\nEXECUTOR OUTPUT:\n{executor_output_json}\n\nORIGINAL TASK PACKET:\n{task_packet_json}\n\nREFERENCE DATA:\n- specs_summary.json (spec requirements by section)\n- partition_schedule.json (assembly details — layers, ratings)\n- door_schedule.json (sizes, ratings, hardware sets)\n- scope_analysis.md § \"CRITICAL FLAGS SUMMARY\"\n- Known discrepancies from takeoff_checklist.md\n\nRUN THESE CHECKS (all of them, every time):\n\n1. UNIT CONSISTENCY\n   - Does the measured unit match the bid breakdown unit?\n   - Is SF used where LF should be (or vice versa)?\n   - Flag: Item 58 (epoxy floor) is LF — verify this is correct\n\n2. SPEC vs DRAWING CROSS-REFERENCE\n   - Does the spec require something not reflected in the quantity?\n   - Example: Spec 092900 requires Type X GWB for fire-rated assemblies — did Executor account for 2 layers?\n   - Example: Spec 093013 says \"no substitutions\" on tile — is the specified product noted?\n\n3. MISSING ITEMS\n   - Is there anything visible on the drawing that has NO corresponding bid line item?\n   - Example: Fire-rated door at Mechanical Room — is it in the bid?\n   - Example: Lead-lined GWB at Panorex room — is it in the bid?\n\n4. CODE COMPLIANCE\n   - Fire-rated partitions: correct UL assembly? Correct number of GWB layers?\n   - ADA: grab bars at all required locations? Clearances?\n   - ICRA: dust control and infection barriers accounted for?\n\n5. MATH VERIFICATION\n   - Recalculate at least 2 quantities independently\n   - Check that room areas sum to approximately suite total (~3,500 SF)\n   - Check that partition SF is reasonable for the suite size\n\n6. KNOWN ISSUES CHECK\n   - Item 51 GWB layer discrepancy — is this addressed?\n   - Fire-rated door missing from bid — is this flagged?\n   - Altro Whitrock vs ceramic tile overlap — is this clarified?\n\nOUTPUT for each item:\n{\n  \"item_number\": <int>,\n  \"verdict\": \"ACCEPT|REJECT|FLAG\",\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"CRITICAL|WARNING|INFO\",\n      \"check\": \"<which check caught this>\",\n      \"description\": \"<what's wrong>\",\n      \"evidence\": \"<spec section, drawing reference, or math>\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"<fix or escalate>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"revised_quantity\": <number or null if ACCEPT>,\n  \"escalate_to_doug\": <boolean>\n}\n```\n\n### 1D. DOMAIN EXPERT (Spec & Code Specialist)\n\n**Role:** Deep-dives into specific technical questions that Planner/Executor/Critic can't resolve. Called on-demand, not every cycle.\n\n**Incentive:** Resolution quality — measured by whether escalated issues get resolved without re-escalation.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Answer specific technical questions about specs, codes, UL assemblies\n- Resolve ambiguities between drawings and specifications\n- Provide manufacturer/product-specific guidance\n- Draft RFI language when clarification needed from architect\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are a Domain Expert for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff. You specialize in {domain}.\n\nQUESTION:\n{specific_question}\n\nRELEVANT SPEC TEXT:\n{spec_excerpt — max 2000 chars}\n\nRELEVANT DRAWING DATA:\n{drawing_notes_or_schedule_excerpt}\n\nPROJECT CONTEXT:\n- Healthcare dental clinic renovation, 5th floor, occupied hospital\n- NYC building code applies\n- Architect: Mancini Duffy\n- Single prime contract\n\nProvide:\n1. ANSWER: Clear, definitive answer with code/spec citations\n2. CONFIDENCE: How certain are you? (high/medium/low)\n3. IF LOW CONFIDENCE: Draft RFI question for the architect\n4. PRICING IMPACT: Does this change the bid quantity or cost? How?\n```\n\n**Domain specializations (spawn the right one):**\n- `fire-protection`: UL assemblies, NFPA 80, fire ratings, firestopping\n- `finishes`: GWB types, tile, flooring, paint systems, healthcare finishes\n- `doors-hardware`: hollow metal, hardware sets, keying, access control\n- `healthcare-code`: ICRA, infection control, ADA, NYC DOB requirements\n- `dental-equipment`: Henry Schein coordination, dental air/vacuum, X-ray shielding\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workflow Pipeline\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    PLANNER                                │\n│  Reads: bid_breakdown.json, takeoff_progress.json        │\n│  Outputs: Task Packets (batches of 5-10 items)           │\n└────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘\n                     │ Task Packet\n                     ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    EXECUTOR                               │\n│  Reads: Drawing PNGs, room/finish/partition/door JSONs   │\n│  Outputs: Quantities + measurement methodology           │\n└────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘\n                     │ Executor Output\n                     ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    CRITIC                                 │\n│  Reads: Executor output + specs + known flags            │\n│  Outputs: ACCEPT / REJECT / FLAG per item                │\n└──────┬─────────────┬──────────────────┬─────────────────┘\n       │             │                  │\n  ACCEPT         REJECT              FLAG\n       │             │                  │\n       ▼             ▼                  ▼\n  ┌─────────┐  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐\n  │ Commit   │  │ Back to  │    │ DOMAIN       │\n  │ to JSON  │  │ Executor │    │ EXPERT       │\n  │          │  │ (retry)  │    │ (on-demand)  │\n  └─────────┘  └──────────┘    └──────┬───────┘\n                                      │\n                               ┌──────┴───────┐\n                               │  Resolved?   │\n                               ├──Yes──┐      │\n                               │       ▼      │No\n                               │   Commit     │\n                               │              ▼\n                               │     ESCALATE TO DOUG\n                               └──────────────┘\n```\n\n### Step-by-Step for a Single Bid Line Item\n\n**Example: Item 50 — W32 partition, 3-5/8\" studs, 1×5/8\" GWB ea side, non-rated**\n\n1. **Planner** creates task packet:\n   - Sheets needed: A-100.00 (construction plan, part3/p12)\n   - Spec sections: 092216 (metal framing), 092900 (GWB)\n   - Context: \"Non-rated partition type W3. Measure total LF from construction plan, multiply by wall height for SF. Note: W2 is the 1-HR rated variant — don't confuse them.\"\n   - Known flags: \"Item 51 has GWB layer discrepancy for W2 type\"\n\n2. **Executor** reads sheet part3/page-12 (A-100.00):\n   - Identifies all W3-type partition segments on plan\n   - Measures linear footage of each segment\n   - Multiplies by ceiling height (likely 9'-0\" or per partition schedule)\n   - Calculates SF for both faces\n   - Output: `{ quantity: 1240, unit: \"SF\", method: \"7 segments totaling 138 LF × 9' height = 1,242 SF, rounded to 1,240\", confidence: \"medium\" }`\n\n3. **Critic** reviews:\n   - ✅ Unit matches bid (SF)\n   - ✅ Math checks out (138 × 9 ≈ 1,242)\n   - ⚠️ WARNING: \"Executor measured total SF but bid may want per-face SF. Clarify: is 1,240 SF one face or both?\"\n   - ✅ No spec conflicts for non-rated assembly\n   - Verdict: FLAG — needs clarification on face count\n\n4. **Domain Expert** (finishes) resolves:\n   - \"Industry standard for partition SF in bid breakdowns: measure one face. The bid line item covers the assembly, not individual faces. 1,240 SF = one face is correct if that's the total surface area of one side.\"\n   - Resolved → Commit\n\n5. **Planner** updates `takeoff_progress.json`:\n   - Item 50: `{ status: \"complete\", quantity: 1240, unit: \"SF\", reviewed: true }`\n\n---\n\n## 3. Adversarial Checks (Critic Agent)\n\n### Check Matrix\n\n| # | Check Name | What It Catches | Example from This Project |\n|---|-----------|-----------------|--------------------------|\n| 1 | **Unit Mismatch** | Wrong measurement unit | Item 58: epoxy floor in LF (should be SF?) |\n| 2 | **Layer Count** | Missing GWB layers in rated assemblies | Item 51: bid says 1 layer, UL requires 2 for 1-HR |\n| 3 | **Missing Scope** | Items on drawings not in bid | Fire-rated door at Mech Room (Door 1 = ¾-HR) |\n| 4 | **Spec Conflict** | Drawing ≠ spec requirement | No-sub clause on tile (093013) — must price exact product |\n| 5 | **Double Count** | Same work counted in two items | Rough carpentry blocking vs. wall protection backing |\n| 6 | **Area Sanity** | Total areas exceed suite footprint | Sum of all floor finishes > ~3,500 SF = problem |\n| 7 | **Code Compliance** | Missing code-required items | Lead-lined GWB at Panorex X-ray room |\n| 8 | **Orphan Items** | Bid items with no drawing reference | Items that can't be traced to any sheet |\n| 9 | **Price Reasonableness** | Quantities that imply absurd costs | 10,000 SF of partition in a 3,500 SF suite |\n| 10 | **Coordination Gap** | MEP/arch conflicts | Dental chair rough-in vs. Henry Schein coordination |\n\n### Cross-Reference Matrix (Critic must verify these intersections)\n\n```\n                    Drawings  Specs   Bid Breakdown  Schedules\nDrawings              —        ✓          ✓             ✓\nSpecs                 ✓        —          ✓             ✓\nBid Breakdown         ✓        ✓          —             ✓\nSchedules             ✓        ✓          ✓             —\n```\n\nFor every item, Critic checks at minimum 2 of 4 sources agree on: quantity unit, scope inclusion, and material specification.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Error Interception Rules\n\n### Decision Tree\n\n```\nERROR DETECTED\n    │\n    ├── Is it a UNIT MISMATCH (SF/LF/EA)?\n    │     └── AUTO-RESOLVE if obvious (e.g., \"LF\" for base = correct)\n    │         FLAG if ambiguous (e.g., Item 58 epoxy \"LF\" — could be either)\n    │\n    ├── Is it a MATH ERROR (wrong multiplication/addition)?\n    │     └── AUTO-RESOLVE: Critic recalculates, Executor retries\n    │\n    ├── Is it a MISSING ITEM (on drawing, not in bid)?\n    │     └── ESCALATE TO DOUG — he decides if it's an RFI or an add\n    │\n    ├── Is it a SPEC vs DRAWING CONFLICT?\n    │     ├── Minor (paint sheen, sealant type): FLAG, note in output\n    │     └── Major (fire rating, structural): ESCALATE TO DOUG\n    │\n    ├── Is it a CODE COMPLIANCE issue?\n    │     └── ALWAYS ESCALATE TO DOUG\n    │\n    └── Is the Executor CONFIDENCE \"low\"?\n          └── DOMAIN EXPERT review before committing\n```\n\n### Severity Definitions\n\n| Level | Definition | Action | Example |\n|-------|-----------|--------|---------|\n| **CRITICAL** | Wrong quantity could lose the bid or create liability | STOP. Escalate to Doug immediately. | Fire-rated door missing, GWB layer discrepancy |\n| **WARNING** | Likely error but bounded financial impact | Domain Expert review, then commit or escalate | Unit ambiguity on rubber base (EA vs LF) |\n| **INFO** | Notable but not blocking | Log it, continue | Paint color code confirmation |\n\n### Auto-Resolve Conditions (no human needed)\n\n1. Math error where Critic's recalculation matches a known reference (room_schedule area)\n2. Obvious unit mapping (e.g., doors = EA, paint = SF, base = LF)\n3. Duplicate detection where one item clearly supersedes another\n4. Rounding differences < 5%\n\n### Escalate-to-Doug Triggers\n\n1. Any CRITICAL severity finding\n2. Spec vs drawing conflict on fire-rated assemblies\n3. Missing bid line item for scope visible on drawings\n4. Healthcare code compliance question (ICRA, X-ray shielding, ADA)\n5. Quantity confidence \"low\" after Domain Expert review\n6. Any item where financial impact > $5,000 estimated\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n\n### 5A. File Structure\n\n```\nhhc-brooklyn-dental/\n├── TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md          ← this file\n├── takeoff_progress.json            ← master tracking (Planner updates)\n├── bid_breakdown.json               ← 298 items (source of truth)\n├── scope_analysis.md                ← project context summary\n├── takeoff_checklist.md             ← gap analysis\n├── agents/\n│   ├── planner_output/              ← task packets per batch\n│   │   ├── batch-001.json\n│   │   └── batch-002.json\n│   ├── executor_output/             ← quantity results per batch\n│   │   ├── batch-001-quantities.json\n│   │   └── batch-002-quantities.json\n│   ├── critic_output/               ← QA verdicts per batch\n│   │   ├── batch-001-review.json\n│   │   └── batch-002-review.json\n│   └── expert_output/               ← domain expert consultations\n│       ├── fire-rating-item51.json\n│       └── missing-door-item44.json\n├── escalations/                     ← items needing Doug's input\n│   ├── gwb-layer-discrepancy.md\n│   └── fire-rated-door-missing.md\n└── final/\n    └── completed_takeoff.json       ← final quantities, QA-passed\n```\n\n### 5B. Session Spawn Commands\n\nEach agent runs as a spawned subagent session. The main session (or a cron trigger) acts as the Planner, spawning Executor and Critic in sequence.\n\n#### Spawn Planner (or run in main session)\n```\nTask: Run the Planner role for HHC Brooklyn Dental takeoff.\n\nRead these files:\n- /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/bid_breakdown.json\n- /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/takeoff_progress.json\n- /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/takeoff_checklist.md\n\nSelect the next batch of 5-10 unmeasured bid items from Doug's direct scope.\nGroup by drawing sheet. Output task packets to agents/planner_output/batch-{NNN}.json.\nUpdate takeoff_progress.json with status \"in-progress\" for selected items.\n```\n\n#### Spawn Executor\n```\nTask: Execute quantity takeoff for HHC Brooklyn Dental.\n\nRead your task packet: agents/planner_output/batch-{NNN}.json\n\nFor each item in the packet:\n1. Read the specified drawing sheet image(s) from /sheets/\n2. Read reference data: room_schedule.json, finish_schedule.json, partition_schedule.json, door_schedule.json\n3. Measure quantities per the Executor prompt template in TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md § 1B\n4. Write results to agents/executor_output/batch-{NNN}-quantities.json\n\nShow your work. Every number needs a derivation.\n```\n\n#### Spawn Critic\n```\nTask: QA review of quantity takeoff for HHC Brooklyn Dental.\n\nRead:\n- Executor output: agents/executor_output/batch-{NNN}-quantities.json\n- Original task packet: agents/planner_output/batch-{NNN}.json\n- specs_summary.json, partition_schedule.json, door_schedule.json\n- scope_analysis.md (Critical Flags section)\n- takeoff_checklist.md (Known Discrepancies section)\n\nRun ALL checks from TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md § 1C and § 3.\nOutput verdicts to agents/critic_output/batch-{NNN}-review.json.\n\nYour job is to find errors. Be adversarial. Challenge every assumption.\nIf something seems too convenient or too round, dig deeper.\n```\n\n#### Spawn Domain Expert (on-demand)\n```\nTask: Resolve technical question for HHC Brooklyn Dental takeoff.\n\nDomain: {fire-protection|finishes|doors-hardware|healthcare-code|dental-equipment}\n\nQuestion: {specific_question}\n\nRead relevant spec section from specs_summary.json (section {NNN}).\nRead relevant schedule from {schedule_file}.json.\n\nFollow the Domain Expert prompt template in TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md § 1D.\nWrite resolution to agents/expert_output/{topic-slug}.json.\n```\n\n### 5C. Orchestration Flow (Main Session Script)\n\n```\nBATCH_CYCLE:\n1. Run Planner → produces batch-{N}.json\n2. Spawn Executor subagent with batch-{N}\n3. Wait for Executor completion\n4. Spawn Critic subagent with batch-{N} results\n5. Wait for Critic completion\n6. Parse Critic output:\n   - ACCEPT items → write to final/completed_takeoff.json\n   - REJECT items → re-queue for Executor (max 2 retries)\n   - FLAG items → spawn Domain Expert OR write to escalations/\n7. Update takeoff_progress.json\n8. If items remain → goto BATCH_CYCLE\n9. When all items complete → generate final summary for Doug\n```\n\n### 5D. Progress Tracking Schema\n\n`takeoff_progress.json`:\n```json\n{\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"total_items\": 298,\n    \"dougs_scope_items\": 82,\n    \"last_updated\": \"2025-07-11T00:00:00Z\"\n  },\n  \"items\": {\n    \"50\": {\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"quantity\": 1240,\n      \"unit\": \"SF\",\n      \"batch\": \"batch-003\",\n      \"executor_confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"critic_verdict\": \"ACCEPT\",\n      \"retries\": 0,\n      \"completed_at\": \"2025-07-11T12:00:00Z\"\n    },\n    \"51\": {\n      \"status\": \"escalated\",\n      \"batch\": \"batch-003\",\n      \"escalation_reason\": \"GWB layer count discrepancy — bid says 1 layer, UL U419 requires 2\",\n      \"escalation_file\": \"escalations/gwb-layer-discrepancy.md\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"complete\": 0,\n    \"in_progress\": 0,\n    \"escalated\": 2,\n    \"not_started\": 296\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Data Hygiene\n\n### The Problem\n\nThe spec PDF is 1,058 pages. The drawings are 86 sheets. Dumping raw content into agent context will:\n- Blow token limits\n- Dilute signal with noise\n- Cause hallucinations from irrelevant text\n\n### Rules\n\n| Rule | Implementation |\n|------|---------------|\n| **No raw PDF text in agent context** | Agents read `specs_summary.json` (pre-extracted, 21 sections with metadata). Full text only via Domain Expert on specific sections. |\n| **No full drawing sets** | Executor gets only the 1-3 sheet PNGs specified in the task packet. Never all 86. |\n| **Summaries over source** | Planner provides 1-2 sentence context summaries per item, not full spec paragraphs. |\n| **Structured data only** | All inter-agent communication via JSON files in `agents/` directory. No prose dumps. |\n| **Context ceiling: 8K tokens per agent** | Task packets, reference data, and instructions must fit in ~8K tokens. If they don't, split the batch. |\n| **Immutable source files** | `bid_breakdown.json`, `specs_summary.json`, schedules — agents READ these, never WRITE. Only `takeoff_progress.json` and `agents/` output files are writable. |\n\n### Pre-Extracted Reference Data (already exists)\n\n| File | Contents | Size | Agent Access |\n|------|----------|------|-------------|\n| `bid_breakdown.json` | 298 line items (no quantities yet) | Small | Planner |\n| `specs_summary.json` | 21 spec sections with titles, page ranges, manufacturers | Medium | Critic, Expert |\n| `room_schedule.json` | 30 rooms with areas and finish codes | Small | Executor |\n| `finish_schedule.json` | Finish types mapped to rooms | Small | Executor |\n| `partition_schedule.json` | Partition assembly details (W1-W4, F1, C00) | Small | Executor, Critic |\n| `door_schedule.json` | 25 doors with sizes, ratings, hardware | Small | Executor, Critic |\n| `drawing_index.json` | Sheet catalog with page references | Small | Planner |\n| `sheet_index.json` | 80 sheets with text previews | Medium | Planner |\n| `scope_analysis.md` | Full project analysis with flags | Large | Planner (full), others (flags section only) |\n\n### What Needs Creating\n\n| File | Purpose | Created By |\n|------|---------|-----------|\n| `takeoff_progress.json` | Master status tracker | Planner (initialize from bid_breakdown) |\n| `agents/` directory tree | Inter-agent communication | First Planner run |\n| `escalations/` directory | Doug review queue | Critic/Expert when escalating |\n| `final/completed_takeoff.json` | Accepted quantities | Orchestrator after Critic ACCEPT |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Known Issues to Pre-Seed\n\nThese issues from `takeoff_checklist.md` should be immediately routed to the appropriate agent on first run:\n\n| Issue | Route To | Priority |\n|-------|----------|----------|\n| Item 51 — GWB 1-HR partition layer count | Domain Expert (fire-protection) → Escalate to Doug | 🔴 CRITICAL |\n| Fire-rated door missing from bid (Door 1 = ¾-HR) | Critic → Escalate to Doug | 🔴 CRITICAL |\n| Item 58 — Epoxy floor unit is LF not SF | Critic → Domain Expert (finishes) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Item 61 — Rubber base unit is EA | Critic → Domain Expert (finishes) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Lead-lined GWB at Panorex room | Domain Expert (healthcare-code) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Altro Whitrock vs ceramic tile scope overlap | Domain Expert (finishes) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Henry Schein dental equipment coordination | Domain Expert (dental-equipment) → Escalate to Doug | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Hazmat/ACM abatement scope unknown (Item 7) | Escalate to Doug directly | 🔴 CRITICAL |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target | Measurement |\n|--------|--------|-------------|\n| Items completed per batch cycle | 5-10 | `takeoff_progress.json` |\n| Critic rejection rate | < 30% (improving over time) | Reject count / total reviewed |\n| False positive rate (Critic flags that aren't real) | < 15% | Doug overrides / total flags |\n| Time to complete Doug's scope (82 items) | 4-6 batch cycles | Progress tracking |\n| Escalations to Doug | < 10 total | Escalation directory count |\n| Zero missed scope items | 0 items on drawings not in bid (after full run) | Final audit |\n\n---\n\n## Appendix: Adversarial Incentive Structure\n\nPer arxiv.org/abs/2601.14351, opposing incentives prevent groupthink:\n\n| Agent | Wants | Opposes |\n|-------|-------|---------|\n| **Planner** | High throughput (more items done) | Critic (slows pipeline with rejections) |\n| **Executor** | High acceptance rate | Critic (rejects sloppy work) |\n| **Critic** | High error detection | Executor (wants clean pass), Planner (wants speed) |\n| **Domain Expert** | Definitive resolution | Critic (may re-flag), Planner (slows queue) |\n\nNo single agent can declare \"done\" — the Critic must approve, and the Critic is incentivized to find problems. The Planner is incentivized to move fast, creating natural tension that surfaces issues early.\n\n---\n\n*This architecture replaces the single-agent takeoff approach. First batch cycle should target Items 8-23 (Demolition) as these are prerequisite to all other quantities.*\n/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/DEMO-TAKEOFF-WORKSHEET.md\n/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md\n/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/scope_analysis.md\n/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/takeoff_checklist.md","cwd":"/home/ec2-user/clawd"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1769918653678}}
